rt the book well  actually I had meant to work on  contemporary post-soviet poetry from  from many years ago in 2002 I had a  Fulbright to Russia I spent a year in  Moscow and was focusing on contemporary  at the time conceptualist post  conceptualist poetry mm-hmm I came back  from that research year and wrote a  master's thesis on conceptualist Russian  poetry but I let it go I felt somehow I  hadn't found my real protagonists yet  and I changed directions with my  research my dissertation and the first  book which she mentioned ended up being  on Nabokov on emigres and transnational  literature's which was something that I  felt I could do well then and in the  context of living and writing in New  York City which I was so it was a while  before I returned to contemporary poetry  as my project when I finished the the  first book mostly  I decided to return to Russia and have  been going back one or two times a year  for summers mostly and it began in 2012  I came to do research on contemporary  poetry and I walked straight into some  of the most exciting protests that I'd  ever encountered this had started in  2011 and was part of the now it's known  as the the long year 2012 immense  political protests all over the country  many in Moscow and Petersburg and I  somehow entered into this met many  people through these protests and I  asked around who were the poets who were  somehow central who were the poets who  were speaking the language who were the  the poets involved with protest culture  most actively I kept hearing the same  names over and over again names like  Keitel Mitzvah for example or Pavarotti  any of the journal Petersburg Journal  tenants niat and I thought okay I think  I found my protagonist and now I can  return to this project okay so because  it's such a press  - concept how did you do the research  for it  my research methods had to change  completely this is the first time that I  was working so actively on living  writers so it's a lot more risky a lot  less safe than working on the bulk of  and so did you get to speak to them  directly  absolutely so I I know and work with all  of these poets which means that my  methods are completely different it's  not archival research poking through  letters but it involves interviews for  the first time for me so I had to  completely retrain to figure out how to  be able to do that and how is that  process for you  a terrifying comment but it felt  challenging but extremely important  terrifying well because of course  there's simply the emotional risks you  can publish an interpretation of a poet  who can then completely disagree with  you or worse yet think that you're a  fool so there's that risk but also for  me I'm very sensitive about not wanting  to engage in something that seems like  intellectual or academic colonialism I  don't want to think about writing on  somehow static subjects but instead I  really try to think about it as a  collaboration with these poets and  writers and interviews really help with  that but also ways of gathering material  are completely different I in some cases  the materials gathered online because  many of these poets use live journal and  Facebook and Twitter and other forms of  disseminating non-traditional forms of  disseminating their work but in other  cases it involves being there being at  the performances where they read their  poems being in protest context to see  how let's say the reading or performance  of a poem galvanized as a crowd how it  how it works in our context interesting  so tell us a little bit about each of  the poet's that you include in the  project okay I'll start why I've  narrowed it down it has been  extraordinarily difficult to narrow it  down but to six poets and there's a  chapter each the way that the book is  currently working two of the poets have  actually been to Yale one kedo madea's  you've who has had an anthology of his  work published in English with n plus  one and ugly duckling press it's called  it's no good and  fantastic that anthology has also  received a great deal of acclaim and key  real madrid is getting really  international attention now he's  publishing also essays political essays  and cultural essays in places like the  new left review and he came to Yale two  years ago to give a reading and I think  that might have been the first  introduction to some of the students and  graduate students here to a this  wonderful figure and he's young and  dynamic and also sings and performs his  poems in a way that you absolutely  cannot be left indifferent when  listening and so that I think was the  the first introduction not only to him  and his poetics but also to the fact  that something extremely exciting and  new was happening in the poetry world in  Russia another poet who came here last  fall was Dimitri Goenka he's also an  academic a theorist and last fall he was  based in Iowa and the prestigious Iowa  Writers Workshop we managed to steal him  for day and a half and he came and gave  a reading and again we had his works in  the original and translation students  were able to meet him and ask him about  how he lives and works and also  questions like does he does he want to  stay in Russia  how does he conceive of that so these  are these are two that I've already been  here and actually this spring I'm  bringing three more in the context of a  conference that I'll mention a little  bit later one of them is a Georgian born  poet female poet Quixote choo Korolev  who writes these absolutely fabulous  poetic dramas that draw attention to and  imagine the voices of the the unheard  and unseen of of Moscow so the immigrant  workers the people who work at the the  cheap marketplaces in Enochs and her  work is absolutely marvelous she's also  an academic a theorist works on  anglo-american poetry which is in  literature which is very interesting  Alexander's kadam is also coming he is a  st. Petersburg institution in his own  righ